\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $E=\bigwedge_{\mathbb C}\mathbb C^n$ be the exterior algebra on $n$ degree-one generators, with graded pieces $E_i=\bigwedge^i\mathbb C^n$. For a homogeneous element $f\in E_d$ define
\[\operatorname{Ann}_E(f)=\{a\in E:af=0\},\qquad (f)=Ef.
\]
If $d$ is odd then $f^2=0$, hence $(f)\subseteq\operatorname{Ann}_E(f)$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every odd-degree form $f\in E_d$, is
\[(\operatorname{Ann}_E(f))_i=(f)_i\qquad\text{for every integer }0\leq i<(n-d)/2?\] \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.01692}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does every odd exterior form have only the annihilators generated by itself below the stated degree threshold?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1500023 (AMR-014-0023). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] R. Fröberg, S. Lundqvist, A. Oneto and B. Shapiro, \emph{Algebraic Stories from One and from the Other Pockets}, arXiv:1801.01692 (2018), §4.4, Problem P. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.01692}.
\end{description}
